 6. Discussion
 
 What the historical optical image does and does not do. In this setting the post-change information
-comes from SAR, and we detected no added change evidence from the pre-change optical image: B scores the
-same with and without it, the model trained only with it scores higher, descriptively, when it is removed, and
-neither is detectably better than a SAR-only U-Net trained with the same recipe (RQ1). Its acquisition timing
-between 1 and 18 months has no detectable effect (RQ2). Two explanations fit, and the design cannot
-separate them: OSCD’s urban changes may be visible in the bitemporal SAR at least as clearly as their
-context is in the pre-change optical image, or the dual-stream network with decision-level fusion may not
-exploit optical context without a post-change optical image. The comparison with Song et al. [2] differs in
-change type, region, sensor configuration and architecture, so our null result does not contradict their
-finding for construction-ready bare land; it shows that it does not transfer to OSCD urban change under this
-protocol.
-
-Why the most recent image scores lowest. An image taken one month before the post-change date
-usually lies after the pre-change date and may already show part of the change. In the pre-change slot such
-an image tells the model that the change was already there. This is a property of the task definition (labels
-between OSCD’s two dates), not of the satellite: a historical image is useful as context only if it precedes the
-change. A study of the gap that avoids the confound needs labels dated at the historical image or images that
-all precede the change.
+comes from SAR, and we detected no added change evidence from the pre-change optical image: B is not
+detectably different with and without it, the model trained only with it scores higher when it is removed
+(exploratory), and neither is detectably better than a SAR-only U-Net trained with the same recipe (RQ1). Its
+acquisition timing between 1 and 18 months shows no detectable linear trend (RQ2). Two explanations fit,
+and the design cannot separate them: OSCD’s urban changes may be visible in the bitemporal SAR at least as
+clearly as their context is in the pre-change optical image, or the dual-stream network with decision-level
+fusion may not exploit optical context without a post-change optical image. The architecture favours the
+second: the optical stream never sees SAR and meets it only at the final 1×1 convolution, so the historical
+image can add only a SAR-independent term to each pixel’s score. A model in which the historical image
+conditions the SAR features, such as early fusion of the stacked inputs, would separate the two explanations;
+we did not train one. The comparison with Song et al. [2] differs in change type, region, sensor configuration
+and architecture, so our null result does not contradict their finding for construction-ready bare land; we did
+not detect it on OSCD urban change under this protocol.
+
+A possible reason for the low 1-month score. An image taken one month before the post-change date
+lies after the pre-change date and may already show part of the change. In the pre-change slot such an image
+could tell the model that the change was already there. This hypothesis is untested: how much change each
+image shows was not measured, and the 3-month image, which also post-dates date 1, gives B’s highest
+score, so scene selection or thresholds frozen on other scenes may also contribute, and the low score is
+concentrated in one city (§4.3). The confound itself comes from the task definition (labels between OSCD’s
+two dates), not the satellite: an image can serve as pre-change context only if it precedes the change. A study
+of the gap that avoids the confound needs labels dated at the historical image or images that all precede the
+change.
 
 Polarisation. For the SAR-only U-Net, VV gives the highest F1 on OSCD, and adding VH, with its lower
