+8. Conclusion
+
+Under one multi-seed protocol on OSCD (9–10 test cities, the official DS-UNet and its U-Net, one recipe, 3-
+month mean Sentinel-1): (RQ1) without the post-change optical image, the historical optical image gives no
+detectable gain, either within the dropout DS-UNet or over a SAR-only U-Net at the registered thresholds;
+(RQ2) the registered test finds no detectable linear trend with its acquisition time between 1 and 18 months,
+and an unplanned comparison finds the 1-month image lowest, concentrated in one city and confounded
+with change already visible in the image, so the experiment does not isolate image age; (RQ3) adding VH
+lowers F1 for the SAR-only U-Net and makes no detectable difference with optical input; (RQ4) without the
+post-change optical image, the asynchronous models are not detectably better than SAR alone and score
+below every baseline that has that image, while with full input the dual-stream DS-UNet scores highest
+across training seeds but its lead over SAR-only is concentrated in one test city (exploratory); (RQ5) the only
+detectable false-alarm difference is in shrubland, 1.3% of the pixels. None of these null results shows that an
+effect is absent. With simulated and composite clouds, a partly cloudy post-change image is worse than a
+missing one; zero-fill under a perfect cloud mask recovers most of the loss for the dropout model, a real
+detector on composites recovers less at low cover, and with a recall-biased mask the dropout model scored
+above a SAR-only fallback rule (exploratory). Whether historical optical imagery helps in architectures where
+it can condition the SAR features, for other change types, with labels dated to separate age from the labelled
+change, or on real cloudy acquisitions, are the main open questions.
 
